WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.668 --> 00:00:06.437
Idiots --

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:08.839
(Laughter)

00:00:08.839 --> 00:00:10.174
Are everywhere.

00:00:10.207 --> 00:00:12.209
(Laughter)

00:00:12.243 --> 00:00:16.614
Some people drive you right up the wall
with their opinions, their attitudes,

00:00:16.614 --> 00:00:17.948
their politics.

00:00:17.982 --> 00:00:20.785
I dealt with all kinds of people
in my 20-plus years

00:00:20.785 --> 00:00:22.553
as a broadcast journalist.

00:00:22.586 --> 00:00:25.156
Eventually, I found
moderating tough conversations

00:00:25.189 --> 00:00:28.426
with whatever kind of difficult people
you just thought of.

00:00:28.459 --> 00:00:30.294
Kind of simple to do --

00:00:30.294 --> 00:00:32.063
not easy -- but simple.

00:00:32.496 --> 00:00:36.000
The trick is to go somewhere
I know you'd rather not be.

00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:38.069
Closer.

00:00:38.102 --> 00:00:40.604
Distance makes things worse.

00:00:41.272 --> 00:00:42.540
I came up in public radio,

00:00:42.573 --> 00:00:46.644
trained by old-school journalists
who taught me the art of neutrality.

00:00:46.677 --> 00:00:49.180
You neutralize your opinions,
deaden your emotions,

00:00:49.213 --> 00:00:52.817
and you can get through most
conversations without rolling your eyes.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:56.387
Generally, it worked, and it built me
a strong reputation for fairness.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:01.892
But when the subject matter felt personal,
neutrality felt phony.

00:01:02.426 --> 00:01:05.696
I used to host a national
talk show on NPR called "1A,"

00:01:05.696 --> 00:01:07.298
and in one of our early programs,

00:01:07.331 --> 00:01:10.668
I interviewed a descendant
of Confederate soldiers.

00:01:10.668 --> 00:01:14.638
He argued that Confederate monuments
should be historically preserved.

00:01:14.638 --> 00:01:18.075
Now as a Black man, I felt
enormous pressure to remain neutral,

00:01:18.109 --> 00:01:22.079
so I decided I was just going to focus
on his arguments instead of on him.

00:01:22.113 --> 00:01:25.049
His logic felt kind of flimsy,
and I called him on it,

00:01:25.082 --> 00:01:27.718
and eventually he just folded and he said,

00:01:27.751 --> 00:01:30.087
“It really doesn’t matter what I say.”

00:01:30.121 --> 00:01:34.158
And the moment he said that,
I knew I had failed.

00:01:34.925 --> 00:01:36.127
I may have had a point,

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.996
but I missed a huge opportunity
to really do my job.

00:01:39.363 --> 00:01:42.833
See, my neutrality allowed me
to interrogate his beliefs,

00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:48.339
but the distance prevented me
from seeing why he believed them.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.109
What if I had asked better questions like:

00:01:52.143 --> 00:01:55.746
"Who influenced you the most
in forming these views?"

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.585
Or, "Suppose we let all of these monuments
and statues stand just as they are.

00:02:01.619 --> 00:02:03.454
What would that mean to you?"

00:02:03.487 --> 00:02:05.723
Getting his story might have shown me him

00:02:05.723 --> 00:02:07.758
behind his canned arguments.

00:02:07.791 --> 00:02:10.494
Grilling him changed nothing.

00:02:10.528 --> 00:02:12.129
It felt pointless.

00:02:12.496 --> 00:02:14.331
When you consider someone problematic,

00:02:14.331 --> 00:02:16.267
there's a better way to deal with them,

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.503
keep your sanity and maybe even connect.

00:02:19.537 --> 00:02:21.238
And it sounds insane.

00:02:21.972 --> 00:02:24.175
Move closer.

00:02:24.175 --> 00:02:26.844
Not to embrace them, but to explore them,

00:02:26.844 --> 00:02:29.046
like a doctor studying a sample.

00:02:29.079 --> 00:02:31.482
You need a microscope, not a telescope.

00:02:31.949 --> 00:02:35.553
Doctors actually helped me learn this
way before I became a journalist.

00:02:35.586 --> 00:02:39.823
As a teenager, I was part
of a summer program for future doctors.

00:02:39.857 --> 00:02:41.392
Clearly it worked.

00:02:41.392 --> 00:02:45.196
And I found myself working
with clinicians studying cancer

00:02:45.229 --> 00:02:47.031
and thrombosis and HIV,

00:02:47.064 --> 00:02:48.432
very serious conditions.

00:02:48.766 --> 00:02:52.236
I kind of naively expected them
to be these crusading scientists,

00:02:52.269 --> 00:02:54.939
but they were just level-headed
people doing their jobs.

00:02:55.372 --> 00:02:58.008
But there was a lesson
in that level-headedness.

00:02:58.042 --> 00:03:01.812
See, I am not neutral
about the cancers in our world:

00:03:01.845 --> 00:03:04.915
bigotry, hoaxes, political violence.

00:03:04.949 --> 00:03:06.350
I want them gone.

00:03:06.383 --> 00:03:08.219
However, as a journalist,

00:03:08.252 --> 00:03:10.888
I often had to deal
with them in my clinic:

00:03:10.921 --> 00:03:12.189
“on-the-air.”

00:03:12.223 --> 00:03:15.893
I survived it by getting as close
to those problems as I could

00:03:15.926 --> 00:03:18.862
and studying them
like samples in a petri dish.

00:03:18.896 --> 00:03:20.164
In other words,

00:03:20.197 --> 00:03:23.500
I had to shift from neutrality

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.536
to objectivity.

00:03:25.569 --> 00:03:27.571
And no, they're not the same.

00:03:28.272 --> 00:03:32.676
Objectivity is just when you park
your emotions and learn without judgment.

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:33.978
I don't have to like it,

00:03:34.011 --> 00:03:35.779
I'm just here to learn from it.

00:03:35.779 --> 00:03:39.783
Neutrality negates your opinions
as if you have no judgments.

00:03:39.817 --> 00:03:43.520
Does anyone here honestly believe
I have no opinions on race?

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:44.688
(Laughter)

00:03:44.722 --> 00:03:45.856
Right?

00:03:45.889 --> 00:03:48.559
Now notice I said park your opinions.

00:03:48.559 --> 00:03:50.394
When you park your car,

00:03:50.394 --> 00:03:52.129
you don't abandon it in the garage.

00:03:52.129 --> 00:03:53.497
You're coming back for it.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:56.834
So you can choose when
to focus inward on what you think,

00:03:56.867 --> 00:03:58.636
or outward on what you can learn.

00:03:59.203 --> 00:04:02.573
Of course, sometimes your car
ain't where you left it,

00:04:02.606 --> 00:04:05.175
and maybe your opinions will move too.

00:04:05.175 --> 00:04:08.112
That maybe is why objectivity
feels so vulnerable.

00:04:08.612 --> 00:04:10.414
That's why it works.

00:04:10.447 --> 00:04:13.284
That vulnerability is a sign of openness,

00:04:13.284 --> 00:04:17.154
and a conversation
that's not open is pointless.

00:04:17.154 --> 00:04:21.525
But if you can stay open,
amazing things can happen.

00:04:21.558 --> 00:04:25.829
Another time on NPR, I was interviewing
a former Baltimore police officer

00:04:25.863 --> 00:04:29.300
who had become this fiery advocate
for law enforcement reform.

00:04:29.333 --> 00:04:31.201
And he was going on and on,

00:04:31.201 --> 00:04:33.237
just a long rant in autopilot,

00:04:33.237 --> 00:04:35.472
just (vocalizing).

00:04:35.472 --> 00:04:38.542
And I was trying to stay objective
and listening as best I could,

00:04:38.575 --> 00:04:42.546
when I thought I heard his voice
waver just a little.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:44.348
And in that waver,

00:04:44.381 --> 00:04:46.583
I detected more than his rage.

00:04:47.084 --> 00:04:48.686
It sounded like trauma.

00:04:49.586 --> 00:04:51.188
So near the end of the interview,

00:04:51.188 --> 00:04:52.656
I mentioned, hesitantly,

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:57.061
that he seemed traumatized
by his experiences as a cop,

00:04:57.094 --> 00:04:58.696
and could he reflect on that.

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:01.765
He said,

00:05:01.799 --> 00:05:05.636
"I put people in prison cells
who are better people than me."

00:05:07.538 --> 00:05:08.739
Whoa!

00:05:09.073 --> 00:05:11.642
Sure enough, he went right back
to his talking points,

00:05:11.642 --> 00:05:13.844
but it didn’t matter,
because in that moment --

00:05:13.844 --> 00:05:15.179
that sliver of his story --

00:05:15.179 --> 00:05:19.583
his defenses dropped, and he shifted
from being right to being real.

00:05:20.784 --> 00:05:23.420
Look, some people are just difficult,

00:05:23.454 --> 00:05:25.589
but we can deal with them more simply.

00:05:25.622 --> 00:05:27.524
Here are three tips to get you started.

00:05:27.524 --> 00:05:29.827
First, get the story,

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:33.797
particularly the key decisions
and the turning points.

00:05:33.831 --> 00:05:38.469
What someone believes is not as important
as how they chose to believe it.

00:05:38.836 --> 00:05:41.305
Second, my NPR host pro tip:

00:05:41.338 --> 00:05:43.574
keep your questions short.

00:05:43.607 --> 00:05:45.709
10 seconds tops.

00:05:45.743 --> 00:05:49.646
Long questions can feel
like a setup or like you're rambling.

00:05:49.646 --> 00:05:52.416
And third, use that mental microscope.

00:05:52.449 --> 00:05:54.284
Look as closely as you can.

00:05:54.318 --> 00:05:57.888
Remember, you don't have to like it,
but learn from it.

00:05:58.756 --> 00:06:01.225
It is time to reconnect,

00:06:01.225 --> 00:06:03.026
even to the people you can't stand.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:06.230
But this time, we're going
to make those connections count.

00:06:06.997 --> 00:06:08.899
You could learn a lot from an idiot.

00:06:08.899 --> 00:06:09.900
(Laughter)

00:06:09.933 --> 00:06:13.904
Especially if you resolve
never to act like one.

00:06:14.938 --> 00:06:17.775
I really appreciate you all
making time for me today.

00:06:17.775 --> 00:06:19.009
Thank you for listening.

00:06:19.042 --> 00:06:21.245
(Cheers and applause)

